        en: "After the death of Charles XII, Sweden made peace with its enemies one by one. Bremen-Verden was sold to Hanover in 1719, and Stettin with southern Western Pomerania went to Prussia in 1720. On 30 August 1721 peace with Russia was signed at Nystad in Finland. Sweden ceded Livonia, Estonia, Ingria, the islands of Ösel and Dagö, and south-eastern Finland with Viborg and Kexholm. Russia returned the rest of Finland, occupied since 1714, and paid two million riksdaler. Shortly afterwards Peter I took the title of emperor. Sweden thus kept most of Finland, as well as Wismar and part of Pomerania, but the leading role on the Baltic had passed to Russia.",
